function renderGlossary() {
  return `
  <p class="lede">Some tables on this page show a range instead of one single number. Here is exactly what that means and how it is calculated.</p>
  <div class="glossary-item">
    <h4>"95% likely range"</h4>
    <p>Some of these percentages come from very few records — for example, only 36 job offers exist in the whole dataset. When a percentage is based on a small number of records, the exact figure could easily have come out differently by chance. This range shows where the true percentage most likely falls.</p>
    <p><strong>How it's calculated:</strong> a standard statistics formula called a <strong>Wilson score interval</strong>. It takes two inputs only — how many things happened (for example, how many offers were accepted) and how many things were possible (how many offers there were in total) — and calculates a range that is 95% likely to contain the real percentage. More records in, narrower range out. Fewer records in, wider range out. This is a well-established method, not a guess and not generated by AI — the formula is public and the code for it is in <code>talentflow/analysis/common.py</code>, function <code>wilson_ci()</code>.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> "72.2% (95% likely range: 56.0%–84.2%)" means: out of the offers we have, 72.2% were accepted, but because there are only 36 of them, the true acceptance rate could reasonably be anywhere from 56% to 84%. Treat 72.2% as an estimate, not an exact fact.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="glossary-item">
    <h4>Why some tables don't have a range</h4>
    <p>A range is only shown for a percentage (something calculated by dividing one count by another). Plain counts — like "how many applications" or "how many days" — don't get a range.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="glossary-item">
    <h4>"Before fixing duplicates" / "after fixing duplicates"</h4>
    <p>6 pairs of candidate records in this data turned out to be the same person, entered twice under a different email. "Before fixing duplicates" counts every record as a separate person. "After fixing duplicates" merges each pair into one person, credited to whichever of their two records was entered first.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="glossary-item">
    <h4>Stage vs. Status</h4>
    <p>Every job application has a <strong>Stage</strong> (where it is in the process — Applied, Interview, Offer, Hired, Rejected, and so on) and a <strong>Status</strong> (just Active or Closed). We found that Stage does not always update correctly — see "How much of the data has an error" in the menu.</p>
  </div>`;
}

function renderSummary() {
